cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the manuscript below."
title: "A two-sided error bound for spectral functions from sample-based quantum diagonalization"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  In sample-based quantum diagonalization and quantum-selected configuration interaction, Rayleigh–Ritz on sampled
  determinants yields the spectral function of a probe, the ground state acted on by an operator. We prove a
  two-sided bound on that spectral function's error. Its relative L1 error on any subspace lies between 1−w, with
  w the probe's captured Born weight, and a bound set by 1−w and the Hamiltonian coupling out of the subspace at
  resolution η, computed from the Ritz pairs, the retained probe and one matrix–vector product per Ritz vector;
  without that coupling the two sides coincide. The coupling term is necessary: an analytic counterexample forces
  any bound indexed on w alone to its trivial value, and on Hubbard rings (L=6, 8) the probe's determinant support
  (w=1) has relative error 0.35–0.43 at η=0.18t, because Rayleigh–Ritz displaces the poles it keeps. In classical
  Hubbard-ring simulations at five sizes up to a sector of 10,306,296 determinants, the bound falls below the
  trivial one at a nearly fixed true error, 2.6×10⁻³–1.5×10⁻² over sixteen (L, η) cells at L=6–12; infinite-shot
  Born-ranked subspaces reach a relative L1 error below 0.05 at smaller sector fractions, where the bound is no
  better than the trivial one. In the determinant basis its coupling term vanishes at w=1 for the local probes
  studied only when every symmetry-allowed determinant is kept (measured at L=6–14). Given the exact ground state,
  it is a certificate for benchmarking at exact-diagonalization scale; a convergence diagnostic must see the
  boundary coupling, not only the captured weight.
